1. TCP/IP (Transmission Control Protocol / Internet Protocol)

TCP/IP poore internet ka main framework hai.

IP (Internet Protocol): Data packets par sender aur receiver ka IP address lagata hai (Routing).
TCP (Transmission Control Protocol): Guarantee deta hai ke data sahi salamat aur order mein pohnche.

Working (3-Way Handshake):
1. SYN (Client requests connection)
2. SYN-ACK (Server acknowledges)
3. ACK (Client confirms, data transfer starts)

Features: Reliable, Connection-oriented. Agar packet lost ho jaye, toh retransmit karta hai.
Use Cases: Web browsing, Emails, File downloads.


2. UDP (User Datagram Protocol)

Transport layer ka protocol jo TCP ke muqable mein fast lekin un-reliable hai.

Features: Connectionless, No delivery guarantee (agar packet drop ho jaye toh dobara nahi bhejta).
Pros: TCP se kafi fast aur lightweight hai kyun ke 3-way handshake nahi hota.
Use Cases: Online Gaming, Live Video Streaming, Voice Calls (VoIP) jahan speed accuracy se zyada zaroori hai.


3. HTTP aur HTTPS (Hypertext Transfer Protocol / Secure)

Web browsing ke liye Application Layer protocols.

HTTP (Port 80)
Client aur Server ke darmiyan plain text mein data transfer karta hai.
Security Risk: Data encrypted nahi hota. Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attack se data chori ho sakta hai.

HTTPS (Port 443)
HTTP ka secure version.
Security: SSL/TLS (Transport Layer Security) ka istemal karta hai. Saara data encrypt ho jata hai.





4. SSH (Secure Shell)

Remote servers ko securely manage karne ke liye protocol.
Port: 22
Purpose: Door baithe Linux/Unix server par secure Command Line Interface (CLI) access deta hai.
Security: Poora session strong encryption (Public/Private Keys) ke zariye secure hota hai.
Use Cases: Cloud servers (AWS, Azure) ki remote administration aur secure file transfer.


5. FTP (File Transfer Protocol)

Ek computer se doosre computer par files transfer karne ka purana protocol.
Ports: 20 (Data Transfer), 21 (Control/Login)
Problem: Standard FTP mein login credentials aur data plain text mein transmit hota hai.
Modern Solution: Aaj kal SFTP (SSH File Transfer Protocol) use hota hai jo FTP ko SSH ki encryption ke sath secure banata hai.


6. Quick Summary

TCP: Port N/A | Web, Email, Files | Guaranteed aur Reliable delivery
UDP: Port N/A | Gaming, Streaming, DNS | Fast, Lightweight, No guarantee
HTTP: Port 80 | Web Browsing | Plain text (Insecure)
HTTPS: Port 443 | Secure Web Browsing | Encrypted (SSL/TLS)
SSH: Port 22 | Remote Management | Encrypted CLI Access
FTP: Port 20, 21 | File Transfer | Upload/Download (Insecure by default)


